WEBVTT

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:02.480
You know, Peter Lynch has always said,

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.600
"Buy a business that's so good that any

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.279
idiot can run it because sooner or later

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:11.040
one will." And well, all I know is if I

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.480
buy the right kind of business at the

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.480
right price with the right people, I'll

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.039
do well over time. You're about to meet

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:18.800
one of the most successful investors in

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.800
the world, Warren Buffett. He gained

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.640
that distinction by shudely investing

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.960
his own and other people's money.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.760
Starting in the 1950s and 1960s, he ran

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.599
an investment partnership that earned a

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:32.079
remarkable annual average return of

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:35.920
nearly 30% per year for 13 years. In

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.280
1965, he paid $12 a share for a company

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.160
named Birkshire Hathaway. In 1995,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.680
Bergkshire Hathaway, which is now an

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.680
investment holding company, was trading

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.000
on the New York Stock Exchange for over

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.520
$25,000 per share. Based in Omaha,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.440
Nebraska, Bergkshire Hathaway owns

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.199
shares of such large companies as

00:00:55.199 --> 00:00:57.840
Coca-Cola, Gillette, and Capital City's

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.960
ABC, as well as 100% of such smaller

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.600
companies as Seas Candies, HH Brown

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.680
Shoes, and a furniture store in Omaha,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.680
Nebraska, named in Nebraska furniture

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.880
mart. In 1994, Forbes magazine listed

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.720
Warren Buffett as the second richest man

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.560
in America. But he's famous for far more

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.720
than just his wealth. His common sense

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.640
approach to business decision-making is

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.720
widely admired both for its wit and its

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.960
wisdom. In what follows, you'll hear

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.040
about how he chooses companies to buy,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.960
about his views on predicting the stock

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.960
market, and about some of his favorite

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.960
people, including Rose Blumin, the

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.119
101-year-old founder of the Nebraska

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:35.280
Furniture Bar.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.360
>> The format's the one he enjoys the most,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.040
a dialogue with a group of students who

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.479
are invited to throw at him any

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:42.799
questions they like. He begins with a

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:44.240
story about near disaster in the

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.000
financial world. It happened when the

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.680
top management of the huge Wall Street

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.840
securities firm of Solomon Brothers in

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.759
which Berkshire Hathaway held a stake

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:53.600
was forced to resign after a treasury

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.840
bond trading scandal. Mr. Buffett

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.439
stepped in as chairman and his first

00:01:57.439 --> 00:01:59.040
challenge was to find the right man to

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.280
run the highly leveraged company and

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.159
fast. I really had a lesson on

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:07.759
unemployment a few years ago. Uh when on

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:12.560
uh August 14th of uh 1991,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.319
I got a phone call at a quarter of 7:00

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:16.160
in the morning,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.760
it was the top management of Solomon,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.560
and they told me at a quarter of 7

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.200
essentially that u

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.520
uh that the head of the Fed of New York

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.200
uh who was the most powerful man in

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.599
finance basically told them that the top

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:34.000
management was unacceptable and that

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.640
therefore uh they were going to offer

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.680
their resignations and uh there was no

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.879
one there to to run the place and that

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:45.519
was a matter of some concern because at

00:02:45.519 --> 00:02:49.120
the time Solomon had balance sheet

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.000
totals of $150 billion

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
of which about 4 billion was equity and

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.800
the other 146 billion was owed to

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.560
someone else

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.239
and that was the largest amount

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:04.879
owed by any American corporation except

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:07.440
for City Corp at that time it's larger

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.120
than the liabilities of the credential

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.959
insurance company uh larger than the

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:13.040
liabilities of the bank of America,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.920
Maril Lynch, American Express, whatever.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.400
Uh and there was one problem about this

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.959
146 billion. A very high percentage of

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:25.360
it came due within a few weeks. And so

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.200
that was the situation. And I went back

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.640
to New York that day on that Friday and

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.840
I met that evening. Uh, and this is this

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.200
is where it may start getting relevant

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.760
to to this group. Uh, one of the

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:38.959
problems I was going to have the

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:40.239
following week was I needed somebody to

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:41.920
run the place. There were 8,000 people

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.480
and this 150 billion of balance sheet

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.959
assets and liabilities and a complex

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.480
business.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.959
And essentially somebody had to be

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:52.879
making decisions minute by minute about

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:54.400
the operations because I was going to

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.920
have to be dealing with regulators and

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.040
and uh uh legislators and people of that

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.360
sort. So, uh, I met 12 people, roughly

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.720
12 that that evening, a dozen or so, and

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.319
I probably knew four or five of them,

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:13.120
uh, by sight. And there were probably

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.720
six or seven that I didn't know,

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.959
although I probably knew their names.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:18.880
So, as I interviewed these people,

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.239
making what for me was the most

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:22.400
important hire by far that I'll ever had

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.120
to make, it it may be of interest to you

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.840
because uh it may not be quite as

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.280
dramatic a time when you get hired, but

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.479
it may be some of the same things going

00:04:30.479 --> 00:04:31.680
through the person that's looking at

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.440
you, the mind of the person that's

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.080
looking at you. Uh the the good news is

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.440
I did not ask them their grades in

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.919
business school.

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:43.840
Uh and the bad news, of course, is I

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:44.800
didn't ask them whether they'd been to

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.759
business school at all. Uh I never I

00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:49.520
never looked at a resume. I it it

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.600
wouldn't have made any difference. I

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.040
knew that all 12 had the IQ

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.800
uh to handle the job. There wasn't no

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.040
one had gotten to that position in the

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.680
firm without being smart enough uh to

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.680
know all the instruments and know how

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.120
markets worked and all of that sort of

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.800
thing. So it wasn't a question of wasn't

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.520
a question of IQ at all. It's just sort

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.360
of hearing how their machinery works.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.240
And uh you can you can tell the people

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.080
that are very full of themselves. I mean

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.919
that

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:19.759
very easy to do. You find a few of those

00:05:19.759 --> 00:05:21.120
in investment banking, believe it or

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.840
not. Uh

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.720
and uh you know, you you could probably

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.639
tech perhaps the ones that aren't going

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:30.479
to have the courage in a in a situation

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:33.360
like that. Uh uh but I wanted the

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.280
qualities, you know, the same thing Ben

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.560
Franklin wrote about a couple hundred

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.479
years ago. the people who don't who are

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:39.600
always willing to give the other person

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.039
more credit than themselves and who

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:42.960
don't cut corners and who who who

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.560
deliver all that promise and a little

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.680
extra and essentially I was very lucky

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.240
because I found I found that individual

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.800
a fellow named Derek Mourn and he the

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.720
next day the board met. We had a lot of

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.919
complications that day and we had a

00:05:57.919 --> 00:05:59.360
group of about 200 reporters probably

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.960
about this size in an auditorium would

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.440
come in on Sunday to cover this story

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.240
and and uh I walked out they assembled

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.520
at about 2:30. I walked out of the

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:08.960
director's meeting about a quarter of

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.039
free. was late because of other problems

00:06:11.039 --> 00:06:12.560
and there had these 12 were and I just

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.520
walked up to this to Derek and I tapped

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.440
them on the chest and I said, "You're a

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.199
towel." And we went right down to the

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:22.800
auditorium and 200 or so reporters fired

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.319
questions at him for 3 hours while he

00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:26.000
sat up at the podium with about 2

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.000
minutes notice that he was going to be

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.080
running the firm. A month later, Derek M

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:33.440
never had asked me what he was getting

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.240
paid. The question of his salary had

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.400
never come up. forget about stock

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.880
options or anything like that. Never

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.360
came up. He just walked right up and

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.120
never never said a word about it and uh

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.919
took on the job. Worked 18-hour days for

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:50.639
months and months and months. Showed

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:52.960
uncanny judgment about what what to

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.319
bring up to me and what not to bring up

00:06:54.319 --> 00:06:56.720
to me and all sorts of things like that.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:58.639
And I often use this illustration with a

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:02.400
with a class is that I say to them if

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.800
you if you were looked under your seat

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.039
today and one of one of you won this had

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:10.000
this uh lucky ticket and the one who had

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.240
that ticket got to pick one of their

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.160
classmates who've been together for a

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.520
couple of years or whatever and you got

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.000
to pick one of your classmates and you

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.840
could pick anyone you wanted to. You had

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:21.840
an hour to make the decision and you

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.520
would get 10% of the earnings of that

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.560
individual for the rest of their life.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.880
And you normally you don't pick the guy

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.960
with the richest father in the class,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.639
but we assume you pick everybody

00:07:32.639 --> 00:07:34.479
starting from scratch. But what would

00:07:34.479 --> 00:07:36.240
you think about in that hour in terms of

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.919
who you picked? Would you think about

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:39.440
who had the highest grades in the class?

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:40.800
Probably not. Would you think about who

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.720
had the highest IQ? Probably not. You

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.280
know, the best looking, probably not. A

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.319
whole bunch of things would go through

00:07:46.319 --> 00:07:48.240
your mind. I think you'd be amazed at

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.479
how most of you would settle on a

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:52.720
relatively few individuals. But the

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:54.240
interesting thing is that when you think

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.720
about what's going through your mind,

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:57.919
you're not thinking about things that

00:07:57.919 --> 00:07:59.360
are impossible for you to achieve

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:00.560
yourself. You're not thinking about who

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.479
can jump 7 feet, who can throw a

00:08:02.479 --> 00:08:05.280
football 65 yards, you know, who can

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.000
recite pi to 300 digits or whatever it

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.240
may be. You're thinking about a whole

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:12.720
bunch of qualities of character. And the

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.720
truth is that every one of those

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.960
qualities is is is is is uh obtainable.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.759
They're largely a matter of habit. Ben

00:08:21.759 --> 00:08:23.360
Franklin and actually my old boss Ben

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.720
Graham. Ben Graham when he was 12 years

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.479
old wrote down all the qualities that he

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:27.840
admired in other people and he wrote

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.759
down the qualities that he found

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:32.000
objectionable and he thought and he

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:33.120
looked at that list and there wasn't

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.479
anything there about being able to run

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:37.039
the 100 yard dash in and 96 or or high

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.519
jumping 7 ft. They were all things that

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:40.880
were simply a matter of deciding whether

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:41.919
you were going to be that kind of person

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:44.399
or not. And when you're young you really

00:08:44.399 --> 00:08:45.920
have that opportunity. as you get older

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.920
it gets tougher because uh somebody has

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.000
said the chains of habit are too light

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.760
to be felt until they're too heavy to be

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.560
broken and in terms of behavior you know

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.279
I see that all the time so I I the time

00:08:57.279 --> 00:08:59.200
to form the right habits is very early

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.600
but it's the the right habits the habits

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.519
that you would time to form the right

00:09:03.519 --> 00:09:05.680
habits is very early but it's the the

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:07.760
right habits the habits that you would

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.360
admire in someone else and want to buy

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:12.160
10% of of whatever individual that is

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.399
they're absolutely obtainable and if you

00:09:14.399 --> 00:09:16.399
had to fall short one other member of

00:09:16.399 --> 00:09:17.839
your class. You know, they're these

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:19.680
these deals always come with a hooker

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.440
and you had to pay 10% of their

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:23.120
earnings. Again, you wouldn't pick the

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.160
person with the lowest grades or

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:25.200
anything. You'd think about the person

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.440
who always cut corners or claim credit

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.720
for things that they didn't do, never

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:30.160
showed up on time. All these little

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.560
things that seem like nothing, but they

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.080
determine whether if you have a 300

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.920
horsepower mo motor that whether you get

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:37.680
300 horsepower of output out of it or

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:39.680
whether you get a whole lot less. And

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.680
the people I see that function well are

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:42.800
not necessarily the people with the

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:43.839
biggest motors, but they're the people

00:09:43.839 --> 00:09:45.920
with the most efficient motors. And it's

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.519
those qualities of character that that

00:09:47.519 --> 00:09:49.040
really make an enormous difference. So

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.839
the what I would suggest to you is think

00:09:51.839 --> 00:09:53.600
about that person you'd buy 10% of and

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.279
the one you'd short 10% of and think

00:09:55.279 --> 00:09:57.680
about why you would do it and then ask

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.240
yourself what on this left hand side of

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.519
the list can't I achieve myself and what

00:10:03.519 --> 00:10:04.880
on the right hand side of the list can't

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.279
I get rid of myself if I possess it and

00:10:07.279 --> 00:10:09.440
in the end you know decide you're going

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.600
to be the person that you buy the 10% of

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.200
that's enough of the sermon I'd like to

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.000
answer questions now and uh and really

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.440
the nastier they are the better I like

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:18.959
it because it's It's it's it's much more

00:10:18.959 --> 00:10:20.560
fun makes it more interesting. So, let's

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.320
start off with one here. Mr. Buffett, I

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.120
was uh reading it sounds like you're in

